Z||000006||JasonS||10-03-2001||07:59 AM||||I was wondering this exact same thing last night.  I went to discount auto parts and was looking through their book of parts.  It has drawings of each radiator that they sell and how many rows or "core's" it has.  If I remember correctly we looked up a corvette LT1 radiator and it was only 2 rows.  I am modifying the front to be able to fit a larger radiator (I love my sawzall).  My spare fiero that I decided to put the v-8 in, I only paid $200 for, so the sawzall option is not for everyone.  I did find some small 4 row rads but they were out of my price range.  I ended up with a 3 row rad from a larger pontiac.  Remember, you can make ANYTHING fit if you have the desire to.  Hope this helps,<P>Jason||||reg||1
Z||000007||artherd||10-03-2001||09:47 AM||||Look for the 'aluminum radiator' thread, I'd love to see these become a popular add-on!  <P>I think they were only $260 or so, for a show-car quality custom fabricated piece.<P>Rest assured I'll be getting one!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/88ttop.jpg">  <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top Metalic Red<BR>88 Formula, Silver<BR>87 Coupe, Metalic Red<BR>"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>                -Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
